    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite passes: **55 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe customerCode collision de-duplication lives in the `assignCode` step of the `POST /api/owners` pipeline, implemented in `AssignCustomerCode.java`. It runs before `save`, so the not-yet-persisted owner is compared only against owners already in the store.\n\nThe step now:\n1. Computes the base code `<REGION>-<HASH8>` as before.\n2. Injects `OwnerRepository` and collects the set of `customerCode`s already assigned to existing owners.\n3. If the base code is free, uses it unchanged. Otherwise appends `-<n>` with the smallest `n \u2265 2` that yields an unused code (`base-2`, `base-3`, \u2026), and sets that de-duplicated value as the owner's `customerCode`.\n\nThis gives the guarantee the spec (and `Cp41Tests`) requires \u2014 distinct owners always get distinct customer codes \u2014 while leaving the stable `<REGION>-<HASH8>` form intact for the common non-colliding case that the other checkpoint tests (Cp09/16/32/14/31/21) assert.\n\nNote: a true hash collision can't be forced through the black-box POST endpoint (it would need an identical normalized-telephone + last-name, which the telephone-uniqueness rule already blocks), so `Cp41` verifies the invariant rather than a literal `-2` suffix. The suffix logic is exercised by the code path and guarded by the existing-code set.",
